AFSCommands: command-level backup, snapshot and restore qualification

Run only as "C:AFSCommands DISPOSABLEVM" on the disposable AFSPROBE: image
with BACKUP/RESTORE authorized by its handler configuration. Required actual
programs in C: are AFSPlusBackup, AFSPlusSnapshot, AFSPlusRestore, AFSPlusClone and AFSPlusReclaim. These
come from afsplus/native/aros/tools and its client library. This probe uses
SystemTags to launch them; it does not call extended-packet functions.
Before writing, the shared identity guard resolves the explicit AFSPROBE
device entry, matches its handler port to GetDeviceProc, and requires
fdsk.device unit91. An assign or other device is refused even when the
DISPOSABLEVM argument was supplied. A same-named volume entry is accepted
only through that separately verified device; its union is never treated
as a startup message.

The probe exclusively creates AFSPROBE:AFSCommands and refuses an existing
root. It captures three files (9000 bytes, 139 bytes and an empty file),
lists the capture through both programs, then changes the live source.
Snapshot VIEW must return the captured binary bytes and directory names.
Restore publishes a fresh file and tree on the same volume; the file's
protection, comment, owner IDs, date and size must match the captured file.
Existing file/tree destinations must be refused without altering their data.
Both decimal and explicit hexadecimal IDs are exercised. Successful deletion
after VIEW and Restore proves their reader leases were closed. A private
AFSCMD assign points to the probe directory. The actual Clone command must
clone its assigned source into its assigned destination, preserve data and
metadata of same-named volume-root shadows, and refuse an existing target.
The assign and root shadows are created only when absent and removed during
the owned cleanup. Reclaim refuses a scope on another handler, drives one-entry maintenance calls
through an in-memory cursor to completion without writing cursor files, and preserves
the published restored descendants and metadata. These command checks do not
inject a retained stage; interrupted-stage reclamation has separate host tests.

Both stdout and stderr are captured in RAM:AFSCommands-output. Expected
refusal diagnostics remain there, while semantic assertions are mirrored to
the console and kernel debug output. The final unique marker is:
 [AFSCOMMANDS] result=PASS files=3 large-bytes=9000 real-cli=5 metadata=1 fresh-targets=1 failures=0
It is emitted only after deleting both captures and all known probe-owned
files/directories. Unexpected remaining children cause cleanup to fail.
AFS-local probes protect already formatted debug records of at most 384 bytes
against local timer interrupts with Disable/RawPutChar/Enable. Formatting and
DOS output remain outside that region; an oversized record emits an explicit
FAIL marker. This is a single-CPU guest guarantee, not cross-CPU serialization.

The source helper's full64-bit capture/list parsing is checked by:
 python3 developer/debug/test/test_afscommands_parse.py
The host check uses ASan/UBSan and rejects overflow, signs, trailing text,
duplicate IDs and malformed records. These are parser checks, not a CLI or
VM runtime PASS. The guest run additionally requires the strict VM harness
normal-termination and fault/provenance checks. It proves neither native M1
behavior nor off-volume archives or system-root rollback. A backup capture
remains on its source volume; restore copies into fresh storage there.
